// The above-ground tissue of a herbaceous plant: `grass.ron`'s
// `shoot_material` *and* its `leaf_material`, which are the same thing here
// and that is the point. A grass has no separate foliage stage — its
// photosynthetic surface **is** its shoot (`plastochron: 0`, the case
// `organism.rs`'s own doc anticipates), so there is no stem for a leaf to
// hang off. WP-B3, `Reports/plant-implementation-plan.md`.
//
// A real material rather than a shade of `leaf`, by this engine's own test
// (`leaf.ron`: "its *physics* genuinely differ on numbers that already
// exist"). Against `leaf` it is lighter, burns faster and hotter for its
// mass, and — the one that matters — breaks into `litter` rather than
// `deadwood`, because a trampled blade is not a snapped branch.
(
    name: "grassblade",
    display: "Grass",
    kind: Plant,
    // Below `leaf`'s 0.25 is tempting and wrong: a blade is thin but it is
    // still turgid tissue, and going under litter's 0.3 would make cut
    // grass float on its own litter layer.
    // Walk-through and climbable, while it is alive. A blade of grass is tissue an animal walks through and a gnome walks past --
    // the same fact `leaf.ron` and `wood.ron` already state about themselves.
    // Left unauthored it made a meadow a wall: measured on `scene=colony`,
    // `grassblade` was 35% of everything still blocking an ant once trunks
    // could be got round.
    //
    // Read only alongside `Cell::organism_id() != 0`, so a painted cell of
    // this material is as solid as it ever was.
    climbable: true,
    density: 0.3,
    // **Grass bends, and this is the number that makes it.** The owner's ask
    // in his own words: *"The system needs to make grass bend and not break.
    // It is ok if the grass gets a little smaller."*
    //
    // A cell leans one cell sideways when its bending moment reaches this,
    // so it is set against the measured moment distribution of a real tuft
    // rather than against anything derived. Censused on `scene=fell
    // species=grass` (`filmstrip`'s "bending moment" line), 43 cells:
    //
    //     p50 0.4   p75 1.4   p90 5.5   p99 12.5   max 19.3
    //
    // 2.0 sits between p75 and p90 -- above the median by five times, so a
    // blade's quiet middle does not creep, and below the loaded end, so the
    // part of a blade that is actually carrying something lies over. Set in
    // the gap rather than on a measured value, per `CLAUDE.md`.
    //
    // **For scale, and this is why the property is opt-in:** a tree's moments
    // over the same census run p50 10.6, p90 572.7, max 15,316. Wood at this
    // stiffness would not bend, it would pour. Wood has no stiffness at all
    // and is rigid by default; when it gets one it will be some thousands,
    // and it will be fitted by the stage that breaks things.
    stiffness: 2.0,
    // **Four bands of four tonal steps**, per `organism::PALETTE_BAND`. The
    // order runs fresh green -> mid -> yellow-green -> straw, so a species'
    // declared range is a contiguous slice of one axis (the same rule
    // `leaf.ron` states) and a stand reads as one sward in a spread of
    // related tones rather than sixteen unrelated draws.
    //
    // Deliberately NOT sharing `leaf.ron`'s greens: a sward that is exactly
    // the canopy's colour is the "tiny trees" failure this package is told
    // to stop and report on. Grass is yellower and lighter than foliage,
    // which is most of what makes a meadow read as ground rather than as
    // very short forest.
    colors: [
        // 0: fresh spring green
        (96, 140, 58),
        (112, 160, 70),
        (82, 122, 48),
        (128, 178, 84),
        // 1: mid meadow green
        (110, 146, 62),
        (126, 166, 74),
        (94, 128, 52),
        (142, 184, 88),
        // 2: yellow-green, high summer
        (140, 162, 68),
        (158, 180, 82),
        (122, 142, 58),
        (174, 198, 96),
        // 3: straw, dry-site and late-season
        (168, 164, 88),
        (186, 182, 102),
        (148, 144, 74),
        (202, 198, 118),
    ],
    // Higher than `leaf`'s 0.75 and it burns out faster still. Cured grass
    // is the fastest fuel in this world by design: it is what lets a fire
    // cross open ground between two stands, which is the whole reason
    // WP-B3's acceptance asks for a paired burn against bare soil and
    // canopy. A grassfire should read as a distinct fast surface regime.
    flammability: 0.85,
    burn_temperature: 520.0,
    burn_duration: 25,
    burns_into: "ash",
    // **Grass throws flame; that is what a grassfire is.** A blade is thin
    // and cured, so it burns out fast (25 frames above) and while it does
    // it is almost all flame -- the fuel itself is a wisp and what a player
    // sees is the fire standing over it. Highest rate of any fuel here for
    // that reason, and it is the reason a burning meadow reads as a front
    // rather than as a row of orange pixels. See `flame.ron`.
    flame_into: "flame",
    flame_chance: 0.18,
    heat_conductivity: 0.2,
    // Trampled, grazed or cut grass becomes ground litter, not a branch.
    // This is the plant side feeding the material the creature branch will
    // eat later, without either side knowing about the other.
    breaks_into: "litter",
    // Both copied from `leaf.ron` and for its reasons, which apply harder
    // here: a blade must not be a load path. `max_unsupported_span: 1`
    // means it holds up nothing but itself; omitting `max_cantilever_reach`
    // opts out of the organism cantilever rule entirely (the default is
    // `u16::MAX`), which is what stops `anchor_support`'s reach measurement
    // amputating a sward. Attachment is still checked separately, so grass
    // whose roots are dug away still goes.
    max_unsupported_span: 1,
    // **The armour table's soft band, on the same argument `leaf.ron` makes
    // and for tissue that needs it more.** A blade of grass is a lamina on
    // a stem a cell wide; it punctures at almost nothing, and it is the
    // tissue an animal walking on the ground actually meets. Left unauthored
    // it sat at the 100.0 default, which prices a meadow like heartwood.
    //
    // Same value as `leaf` and `moss` -- this is soft tissue and the three
    // are not meaningfully different to a mandible.
    penetration_resistance: 0.1,
)
